@3.4 Strings
.
=3.4 STRINGS
.
=WHAT IT COSTS
.
.  ASM: GP.INSTR GP.STRPTR GP.COMP
.    ASM -- a keyword, run by machine code in the GP runtime block. A program
.    using ANY GP keyword pays for the whole block.
.  COMPOSITE: GP.CONTAINS GP.ISEMPTY
.    COMPOSITE -- a keyword with no machine code of its own. The compiler
.    expands it into keywords that already exist, so it costs the runtime
.    nothing at all.
.
=DESCRIPTION
.
.Five keywords. GP.INSTR is the only string search GPC has; without it there
.is none.
.
.Trimming, padding, splicing and case folding are modules, not keywords:
.STRCASE.INC.BL (SEC 4.8) for case, STRINGS.INC.BL (SEC 4.2) for everything
.else. They cost p-code only in the programs that #INCLUDE them and nothing
.in the GP block. GP.STRPTR is the keyword they are built on.
.
.The in-place routines take an ADDRESS, GP.STRPTR(a$), and never a literal:
.GP.STRPTR("hello") is an address inside the p-code, so trimming it edits the
.running program. Case conversion leaves digits, punctuation and PETSCII
.graphics unchanged.
.
.There is no GP.PAD. In-place work cannot grow a string past the capacity it
.was created with, which is what decides which side of STRINGS.INC.BL a
.routine lands on: the pads and STR.SPLICE grow, so they are BASIC
.assignments and reallocate; the trims only shrink, so they are assembly.
.
=IN THIS SECTION
.
>23|3.4.1 GP.INSTR -- position of a substring
>24|3.4.2 GP.CONTAINS -- test for a substring
>25|3.4.3 GP.ISEMPTY -- test for a zero-length string
>26|3.4.4 GP.COMP -- compare two strings, ignoring case
>27|3.4.5 GP.STRPTR -- address of a string block
.
=EXAMPLE PROGRAM
.
.  STRINGS.EXP.BL -- runnable, in the GPC-BASIC folder
.
=SEE ALSO
.
>57|4.8 STRCASE.INC.BL -- case, in place
>48|4.2 STRINGS.INC.BL -- string helpers
